  "description": "A fast breakdown of Dendrites: Signal Receivers for builders.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Some neurons have dendrites so elaborately branched they look like coral reefs under a microscope.",
      "visual": "🪸"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Each branch is covered in tiny spines—small bumps that form synaptic connections with other neurons.",
      "visual": "🔗"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "A single neuron can have thousands of dendritic spines, each receiving a different signal.",
      "visual": "📡"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Spines can grow or shrink based on activity—learning physically changes dendritic shape.",
      "visual": "🌱"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "In Alzheimer's disease, dendritic spines wither before neurons die, silencing connections first.",
      "visual": "🍂"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Dendrites don't just receive signals. They physically reshape to receive better ones.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What are dendritic spines?",
    "options": [
      "Small bumps that form synaptic connections",
      "Protective coatings on axons",
      "Energy-producing organelles"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
